    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "401cd3c5_b49fda30",
        "filename": "/COMMIT_MSG",
        "patchSetId": 6
      },
      "lineNbr": 9,
      "author": {
        "id": 1882526
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-02-09T20:16:37Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "+@jaewan@google.com, who might provide valuable insights given his recent DT work.\n\n---\n\n\u003e The rationale for creating a separate node for host-controlled properties is not security but ABI.\n\nAgreed.\n\nHowever, introducing a new node conflicts with another (arguably unwritten) ABI design principle that all AVF-related entries (which some would say is already an abuse of the DT) would be grouped in this single `/avf` node.\n\nYes, we already messed up with the `/chosen/avf,...` props but these are good reminders that such ABIs are \"forever\".\n\n\u003e In theory, reference tree  could have a node [...] causing unnecessary conflicts\n\nThat\u0027s correct but `/avf-untrusted` doesn\u0027t prevent that, as Alan said.\n\nWith the \"single DTBO\" implementation, host userspace will detect those conflicts before spawning the guest, hopefully failing its boot. In turn, this should be detected by our CTS, with the appropriate coverage, flagging any such improper integration done by vendors.\n\nFYI, the VM DTBO (for device assignment) also allows vendors to insert (trusted) entries at arbitrary paths into the guest DT.\n\n\u003e the hack/solution to similar problem in ABL - it adds the relevant data only in `/avf/reference/avf` to avoid conflicting with other device nodes\n\nThe fact that a VM reference DT can be used to patch any part of the guest DT isn\u0027t a \"problem\", it\u0027s a design choice which might save us down the line, if necessary to support some use-cases. Jaewan wrote aosp/2919550 following this ABI.\n\nOTOH, gpar/787918 only implemented a subset of the ABI, not due to some concern about conflicting nodes, but because of short deadlines + not having use-cases requiring the \"full\" ABI: that\u0027s why I called it a \"hack\". Anyhow, a vendor-specific implementation detail should in no way define or be used as the reference for an Android ABI.\n\nSo I don\u0027t see any pros for defining `/avf-untrusted` over `/avf/untrusted` and would recommend we use the latter, for clarity and namespacing.",
      "parentUuid": "372d6074_03e003b7",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 9,
        "startChar": 32,
        "endLine": 9,
        "endChar": 45
      },
      "revId": "657338d2c3112bc847979eccc128e7f6ef0854ee",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "a3ad85e0_80bb7a9e",
        "filename": "/COMMIT_MSG",
        "patchSetId": 6
      },
      "lineNbr": 9,
      "author": {
        "id": 1897307
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-02-09T21:59:13Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Hang on, just checking if we are concerned about the same `ABI` - it is the guest OS\u0027 (which are part of Android system) expectation about device tree (which, while maybe be set by crosvm(/system), is heavily influenced by vendor related partitions such as ABL/pvmfw).\n\nThe expectation is also strongly around trustworthiness of the nodes. (How u-droid blindly trusts value in /chosen/avf,strict-boot is a good example).\n\nIn the current model, it looks like everything within the device tree received by the guest is trusted. (How it is done is implementation detail, reference dt, pvmfw sanitization and what not).\n\nSo, the change in the ABI introduced due to ABI will be roughly from:\n`Trust whole DT` -\u003e `Trust whole DT except /avf-untrusted or /avf/untrusted`. I\u0027m cool with either change as far as this this is concerned.\n\nWe\u0027re also concerned about the (the weaker) ABI boundary between pvmfw/ABL/crosvm (afterall, we ship the reference impl of pvmfw \u0026 that hopefully should be adopted as is). This, I believe doesn\u0027t require any change, pvmfw will do a very lightwt. validation of the node /avf/untrusted (b/324046698) [This will become more relevant if we make use of `Id` \u0026 other nodes in `/avf/nonsecure` in pvmfw].\n\nLets circle back on this after Korea is back from holidays.",
      "parentUuid": "401cd3c5_b49fda30",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 9,
        "startChar": 32,
        "endLine": 9,
        "endChar": 45
      },
      "revId": "657338d2c3112bc847979eccc128e7f6ef0854ee",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "0fbc8677_5dd0cbd6",
        "filename": "/COMMIT_MSG",
        "patchSetId": 6
      },
      "lineNbr": 9,
      "author": {
        "id": 1882526
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-02-12T13:22:37Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I think we agree but, to summarize: we\u0027ll introduce the `/avf/untrusted` guest DT node as\n\n1. All AVF bits-and-pieces are grouped under the single `/avf` node\n2. Any host-provided flag with a value that cannot be verified by pvmfw and which is specific to AVF (i.e. doesn\u0027t concern the virtual platform itself) goes under `/avf/untrusted`. This path acts as a hint/reminder for guest userspace that these values need to be treated with care and properly sanitized (e.g. no assumptions about value lengths).\n\nand the Id will be the first property we pass through that node, correct?\n\nFeel free to resolve if this matches your understanding.",
      "parentUuid": "a3ad85e0_80bb7a9e",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 9,
        "startChar": 32,
        "endLine": 9,
        "endChar": 45
      },
      "revId": "657338d2c3112bc847979eccc128e7f6ef0854ee",
